@@ -0,0 +1,180 @@
+# Temporal Authority semantics and io_uring personalities
+
+## Governing contract
+
+**A_out ⊆ A_in**
+
+For each transformation, compare the output with the effective, legitimately
+supplied and delegable inputs **at that transformation's creation point**.
+Inputs include provenance, explicit rights/scopes, source capability state,
+applicable LSM constraints and expressly authorized grants/delegations.
+Legitimate delegation does not require attenuation: preserving all legitimately
+supplied authority is valid. Every future class must demonstrate the same
+invariant with reproducible lifecycle, temporal, effective-restriction and
+concurrency tests; see [regression requirements](FUTURE-REGRESSION.md). Identity
+alone is not an input grant. An absent/unhandled class is not zero Linux power:
+it intentionally continues native authorization. A zero-right file capability
+is not the same thing as an unhandled task domain.
+
+**A later reduction of the source's Authority does not retroactively invalidate a previously legitimate independent capability.**
+
+**A historical identity or credential snapshot is not automatically equivalent to a legitimately delegated Authority capability.**
+
+T1: source A has READ|WRITE and legitimately creates independent B with both.
+T2: A reduces to READ; B still has READ|WRITE. T3: deriving C **from A** cannot
+produce WRITE. Deriving from independently owned B may still produce WRITE;
+B, not A's current task domain, is then the actual input. This is not a hidden
+ceiling or spontaneous restoration. No independently authorized grant may be
+smuggled into an attenuation operation without identifying its provenance.
+
+These rules concern the capability/context that is supplied, not an immutable
+listing of every pathname that might ever exist. Directory scopes authorize
+future descendants according to their existing object/mount semantics. Native
+DAC and LSM policy remain independently evaluated; policy changes are governed
+by their own authorization/revocation contracts, not silently frozen forever.
+
+## Independence is not descriptor numbering or necessarily deep copying
+
+| Operation | Actual ownership/sharing | Temporal check / publication point |
+|---|---|---|
+| F_GETFD_CAP | Observation of mode-derived or managed bitmap; not a grant or complete effective-LSM-permission report | Bitmap/mode read; output is a point observation, not an entitlement to later mint those bits |
+| F_RESTRICTFD_CAP | Changes the same open file description's mutable ceiling | Initial pointer cmpxchg publication or atomic AND; every dup/SCM/fork alias observes that ceiling |
+| F_CLONEFD_CAP | New struct file and private capability bitmap; saved source opener, independent subsequent offset | Source rights read supplies the ceiling; source/request/mode intersection plus independent inode/open/post-open/clone LSM checks before fd_install |
+| dup/F_DUPFD/forked FD/SCM_RIGHTS/pidfd/fixed io_uring slot | References to the same struct file, not independent attenuation state | Reference/import boundary plus applicable Linux/LSM transfer checks; later restrictions of that file propagate |
+| Mapping | Separately established VMA, not a live rights-bitmap alias | mmap/mprotect checks; existing VMAs are not retroactively revoked by file attenuation |
+| Add scope | Privileged explicit grant rooted at pinned path; new immutable Authority state | CAP_SYS_ADMIN authorization and successful credential commit; post-confinement addition remains forbidden |
+| Confine/drop scope/drop right | Replaces this task's credential/Authority snapshot; no global mutation of old snapshots | commit_creds publishes the new task state; handled/sealed monotonicity and subset checks remain |
+| fork credentials | Referenced/copied credentials retain immutable Authority; independent later per-task replacement | copy_creds/prepare_creds; parent reduction does not mutate child's earlier context |
+| execve | Ordinary credential lifecycle, FD inheritance/CLOEXEC and LSM checks | Credential commit; confined authority_cred_clear retains domain; no new file-cap rights from exec |
+| io_uring personality registration | Explicit ring-bound execution-context delegation holding a referenced complete credential, including immutable Authority | get_current_cred capture and successful xarray publication under uring_lock |
+| Personality use | Request acquires its own credential reference, subject to LSM adoption and operation checks | Serialized personality lookup/get_cred; later execution uses that captured request context |
+| Personality unregister | Removes shared ring namespace membership, not global revocation | xa_erase under uring_lock; future unresolved ID use fails; prepared requests/open FDs retain their legitimate references |
+
+The FD clone copies the initial file position; subsequent offset changes are
+independent. A source descriptor number and an imported/duplicated number are
+not evidence of independent state. Immutable objects may be physically shared
+by reference while their holders own independently valid historical contexts:
+copy-on-write replacement, not pointer inequality, establishes independence
+from later changes to the original holder.
+
+No current API implements global/object-level revocation of already issued
+capabilities. Unlink, close of one alias, local domain reduction and personality
+unregister must not be relabeled global revocation. A future revocation design
+would need explicit target/lifetime/ordering rules and separate tests. The
+planned `--no-delegation` flag is **not implemented**; no test assumes it exists.
+
+## Precisely recognizing personality registration as delegation
+
+The selected contract recognizes **successful explicit IORING_REGISTER_PERSONALITY**
+as creation of a ring-bound execution-context capability from the registering
+subject's current legitimate context. This is not a blanket rule that any
+copied identity or arbitrary saved pointer becomes Authority. Required evidence
+is actual complete-context capture, valid ownership, lifetime retention, bounded
+use and independent restrictions. The ordinary Linux registration mechanism
+already implements those properties in this prototype; no new UAPI or Core
+feature is introduced by recognizing it.
+
+The effective handle is access to the ring plus its registered ID. IDs are not
+secret/global capabilities. Registration into an already-shared ring delegates
+the context to other eligible ring holders, subject to ring restrictions and
+LSM adoption checks. Applications must include rings/personalities in capability
+hygiene. Removing pathname scopes does not revoke those earlier delegations.
+A launcher needing a smaller total authority must not retain or accept a broader
+registered context/broker unintentionally. This does not establish a universal
+process sandbox or add process/IPC/network authority classes.
+
+### Source-level provenance answers
+
+1. `io_uring/register.c:io_register_personality()` calls `get_current_cred()`
+   and publishes that pointer with `xa_alloc_cyclic`; allocation failure puts
+   the reference. It captures the **registrar**, not the ring creator.
+2. There is no new allocated `struct authority` at registration. The logical
+   execution-context capability is independently owned by the ring reference;
+   it references immutable state, not the source task's future mutable slot.
+3. It is not UID/GID-only capture. `struct cred` contains `authority` plus native
+   credentials/security context; `prepare_creds()` calls `authority_get()` and
+   `put_cred_rcu()` calls `authority_put()`. Holding the credential consequently
+   holds Authority, scopes and their path references alive.
+4. Confinement/scope reduction prepare and commit replacement credentials and
+   cloned Authority. They do not edit the personality's old context.
+5. Registration restrictions are the implemented ring access/registration
+   policy and issuer rules, allocation checks and any existing independent
+   Linux restrictions. There is no extra Authority no-delegation rule. Privileged
+   scope construction remains separately authorized, never implied by an ID.
+6. At SQE preparation, `io_init_req()` resolves the personality, gets a request
+   reference and invokes `security_uring_override_creds()`. SELinux checks
+   IO_URING__OVERRIDE_CREDS; AppArmor checks the corresponding mediated class.
+7. `__io_issue_sqe()` overrides **subjective** credentials for the operation and
+   reverts them afterward. The objective task domain remains confined. Ordinary
+   open/namei/Authority checks execute with the selected saved subject; the old
+   unhandled context is not a zero-right context. Defaults do not inherit the
+   ring opener's broader credentials: async preparation captures the issuer.
+8. Registration after confinement or reduction captures the new bounded state.
+   Retained UID 0 cannot restore omitted handled rights or widen object scope.
+   A prior scoped READ-only personality cannot perform WRITE or open outside
+   its captured scope even if the source later loses all current scope rights.
+9. LSM semantics are not universally identical. Captured Landlock restrictions
+   travel with its referenced credential; relevant current SELinux/AppArmor
+   adoption vetoes remain active. Saved-state file denials remain active during
+   issue. Policy reload/global revocation remains the independent LSM's contract.
+10. `io_unregister_personality()` erases and puts the ring's reference; request
+    cleanup puts its own reference. `io_ring_ctx_wait_and_kill()` walks and
+    unregisters remaining entries under uring_lock, then asynchronously tears
+    down the ring. Aliases keep the same ring alive. Unregister is not evidence
+    of a leak when an already prepared request legitimately retains a reference.
+
+## Concurrency and atomicity boundaries
+
+Personality registration/unregister, request initialization and relevant issue
+paths serialize the ring registry with `ctx->uring_lock`. A request lookup that
+successfully acquires its reference before erasure remains valid afterward;
+future lookup of that now-absent ID fails. IDs may eventually be reused, but a
+new ID binding uses the new registrant's context, not stale authority.
+
+A task cannot concurrently execute its own registration and credential-changing
+syscall; other threads are distinct subjects. Current confinement additionally
+rejects a live multithreaded task with EBUSY. The test deliberately races the
+attempt, confirms no state change, and enters after the peer exits. A peer that
+already inherited an old valid context may legitimately register **its own**
+context; it is not minting from another task's later reduced state.
+
+FD reduction linearizes at capability-pointer publication/atomic AND. Clone's
+bitmap ceiling linearizes at the source-rights observation: a clone overlapping
+reduction may retain an earlier valid snapshot; one started after completed
+reduction must not recover dropped bits. Independent LSM/open checks still
+apply before publication. There is no claimed single global atomic transaction
+covering concurrent LSM policy reloads, namespace changes and every cache;
+each independent layer's synchronization/revocation contract remains relevant.
+
+## Qualification and classification
+
+**EXPECTED_VALID_DELEGATION** is the classification of the reported pre-confinement
+personality finding. It is supported by both complete-context ownership in the
+source and explicit scoped RW/READ/zero tests, not inferred merely from a
+saved UID or from native Linux behavior. The prior unscored observation is
+superseded only as a contract ambiguity; its raw behavior/logs are preserved.
+
+The focused test distinguishes independent FD clones, shared file aliases,
+per-task COW contexts, native credential delegation, source attenuation,
+asynchronous issue, prepared-request lifetime and unimplemented revocation.
+Dedicated enforcing LSM tests check authorized adoption, current adoption veto,
+and a saved file-denial state. AppArmor needs an explicitly selected policy ABI
+containing io_uring mediation; the initial default-ABI failure remains recorded,
+not converted into a kernel pass by changing its assertion.
+
+No production code or credential rebasing changed. Focused results and hashes
+are in `/tmp/authority-v1-final/temporal-authority/REPORT.md`. This resolves the
+personality temporal-semantics blocker within the stated configurations. At that
+temporal checkpoint whole-v1 remained NO-GO; the subsequent source-based KCSAN
+attribution decision is CONDITIONAL GO under the limits retained in the
+[frozen experimental baseline](FILESYSTEM-V1-BASELINE.md). This does not claim
+the underlying races are harmless or the kernel sanitizer-clean.
+
+## Extension boundary
+
+The same **A_out ⊆ A_in** rule governs future supported transformations/classes.
+Restricted kernel extensions remain long-term work: native kernel modules run
+inside the privileged kernel TCB. Meaningful restriction of untrusted native
+code needs execution/memory isolation not supplied by the current Core. No
+restricted-module, Rescue Authority, global sealing/revocation or permission
+CLI implementation is introduced here.
